WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.780
For all of the can't-miss TV going on, there
are lots of series critics say you can go

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:05.620
ahead and skip.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.100
The following shows are our picks for the
very worst of 2018.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.700
ABC promoted this midseason replacement as
the triumphant return of Zach Braff, who was

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.660
away from TV for almost a decade after Scrubs
finished its long run.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.360
What those ads didn’t include was a discussion
about what Alex, Inc actually is.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.320
It would appear to be a typical family comedy
about a goofy dad, his long-suffering wife,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.579
and their kids, but it’s also about a guy
who has a midlife crisis, starts his own company,

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.800
and documents the journey with a podcast.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.780
Based on the true story of StartUp host Alex
Blumberg, Alex, Inc. brings the thrill of

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.440
watching people talking into microphones to
the visual medium of television.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.660
It’s about as exciting as it sounds, especially
since everyone on the show treats Alex like

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.820
he's an absolute genius for deciding to do
a podcast.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.540
“Nobody thinks radio is cool, but when you
do it, it’s amazing.”

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.120
Who doesn’t love people who do podcasts
talking about how smart they are because they

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.120
do podcasts?

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.100
Remember that Disney movie, Max Keeble's Big
Move, about a middle schooler who pranks all

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.450
his bullies when he learns his family is moving,
but then has to deal with all the consequences

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:15.940
when they don’t actually move?

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:21.200
The CW's breezy dramedy Life Sentence has
that same premise, but replaces schoolyard

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.120
pranks with the much more tragic subject of
cancer.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.300
A young woman named Stella thought she was
dying and for years lived every day like it

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.080
was her last, only to be cured and left with
the consequences of her impulsive decisions.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.310
If you enjoy getting some laughs out of a
character being humiliated for thinking she

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.100
was going to die, Life Sentence is the show
for you.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.240
There’s rarely a shortage of medical dramas
on TV, so if a new one is going to stand out,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.000
it has to give viewers a hook early.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:52.100
For The Resident, that involved establishing
its lead character, Dr. Conrad Hawkins, as

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:57.119
a medical rogue who plays by his own rules
by having him pour out an energy drink on

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:01.990
someone’s car and then peel off his shirt
to show a hilariously massive “Death Before

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:02.980
Dishonor” tattoo.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.860
Sadly, that’s about as much character development
as you get before the show starts asking you

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:12.870
to side with Hawkins as he makes rash decisions
and ignores his superiors because he has a

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:16.140
gut feeling about what crazy medical procedure
to go for.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:21.120
He doesn't come across as heroic, just arrogant
and smarmy, a standard-issue hard-to-like

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.370
anti-hero story like House without the charm,
or Mad Men without the style.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.240
“I have only one rule, covers everything
…I’m never wrong.”

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.360
Star Matt Czuchry has plenty of talent and
intensity, but since Grey’s Anatomy felt

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:38.569
fresher at the start of its 14th season than
this show does in its first, it looks like

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:39.560
The Resident is terminal.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.860
Splitting Up Together is familiar in that
it's a family sitcom about overwhelmed parents

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:45.800
raising their kids.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.940
It does, however, offer up a twist we haven’t
seen before.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.260
Mom and Dad are in the process of getting
a divorce, but rather than have one parent

00:02:53.269 --> 00:02:57.430
move out, both parents stay in the house and
keep separate living quarters to provide their

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:59.360
kids with a stable home situation.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.540
While that's a very modern and true-to-life
situation, it's a hard sell for a silly little

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.040
sitcom.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.620
Considering that TV audiences mostly know
star Jenna Fischer from watching her slow-burn

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:14.049
romance as Pam on The Office, seeing her quip
through the collapse of a marriage is just

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:15.640
a little too depressing for laughs.

00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:21.260
Here’s something that sounds like a fever
dream: Co-created by Oscar-nominated actor,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:26.379
Empire star, and Hollywood oddball Terrence
Howard and his wife, Fright Club is a hybrid

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:31.280
of a reality prank show and a dark comedy
with a horror movie premise: Howard invites

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.160
his friends, and a fan who thinks she won
a contest, to a spooky haunted estate in the

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.980
spooky haunted estate capital of America,
New Orleans.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.100
Then Howard and his cohorts scare their victims
with elaborate schemes in the form of swamp

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:49.010
monsters and gory, realistically rendered
voodoo rituals before gently letting his victims

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.060
know they’ve been pranked.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.520
The audience, however, had no such reveal.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.420
Believe it or not, this show was actually
real.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:01.200
Ricky Gervais is a TV legend for co-creating
and starring in the original British version

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.520
of The Office, but his latest project isn't
a single-camera comedy or nuanced character

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:07.020
study.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.560
Instead, Child Support is basically a more
convoluted, humiliating version of Are You

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.240
Smarter Than a Fifth Grader.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:19.100
See if you can follow this bizarre setup:
In a TV studio, host Fred Savage asks an adult

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.000
contestant a trivia question.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:25.380
If they answer correctly, their response is
compared to the answer given by a member of

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:30.100
a panel of adorable children who were asked
the same questions by Gervais in what looks

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.220
like a focus group situation in a creepy conference
room.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.680
If the kid answers correctly, the adult player
stays in the game.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.880
If the adult was wrong, they can save themselves
if a kid panelist answers correctly; if the

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.860
kid is wrong, too, then the adult is eliminated.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.670
Exactly the kind of simple, easy-to-follow
setup that a great game show needs.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:49.480
Or not.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.180
“This is worse than the Golden Globes.”

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.340
Maybe Gervais thought a game show about children
should feel like it was designed by one.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.280
All in the Family was among the most popular,
and controversial, shows of the 1970s.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.340
Through Carroll O'Connor's portrayal of Archie
Bunker, a stubborn conservative at best and

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:10.190
unrepentant racist at worst who was constantly
arguing with his son-in-law, writers could

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.080
debate the social issues of the day.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:17.340
In 2018, the CBS sitcom The Neighborhood obviously
emulates that classic formula.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.820
It's about a middle-class white couple who
move into a predominantly African-American

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:26.070
area, where they’re happily oblivious to
the challenges their neighbors face until

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.000
they're called out on it by their new neighbor.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:32.460
It’s one thing to never quite get to the
provocative, progressive territory of All

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.680
in the Family, but with formulaic and frankly
boring stories, it doesn’t even come close

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.780
to contemporaries like Blackish, either.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:45.220
"Oh boy oh boy they weren't kidding when they
said you guys were were not from around here."

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.900
There's certainly nothing wrong with a TV
series that aims to tell relatable truths

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:54.790
about contemporary life, but sometimes a show
is a little too devoted to encapsulating a

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:59.060
realistic experience, and becomes an annoying,
unpalatable slog.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.020
I Feel Bad is that kind of show.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.220
Feeling like a retread of countless other
"stressed mom" sitcoms like Roseanne or The

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:10.320
Middle, I Feel Bad is about a video game artist
who constantly wonders, often aloud, if her

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.070
work life suffers because of her family commitments,
and if her family life suffers because of

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:16.310
her demanding job.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:21.070
Oh, and if those weren’t enough cliches
for you, don’t worry: I Feel Bad also adds

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.220
in a dopey husband and meddling parents.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.500
It’s just a shame they forgot to include
things like good jokes, and well-developed

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:28.000
stories.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:33.460
Obnoxious and self-centered people who have
no idea how loathsome they are can be prime

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:37.470
targets for comedy, but what happens when
the writers don’t seem to have any clue

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.820
that their characters are unwatchably awful?

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:44.460
It turns out, what happens is HBO’s Camping,
from Lena Dunham and Jenni Konner.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.400
The eight-episode show agonizingly takes place
over just a single weekend.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.010
It follows a birthday for a whiny suburban
milquetoast named Walt, played by Doctor Who's

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:57.540
David Tennant, that's planned down to the
minute by his wife Kathryn, played by Jennifer

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.040
Garner.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:02.820
They're joined by a series of annoying friends
and associates played by hard-trying, well-liked

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:06.910
actors as they swim, hang out, and passive-aggressively
gripe at one another.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:10.760
It’s all the fun of hanging out with people
you hate for an entire weekend, but with the

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:14.350
added twist that everyone involved is getting
paid for it except you.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:19.240
The world's most famous Baldwin brother has
done pretty much everything you can do on

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:24.191
television, from sitcoms like 30 Rock, soap
operas like Knots Landing, game shows on the

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.440
revival of Match Game, and even frequent appearances
on Saturday Night Live.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:33.260
The only thing he had left was a talk show,
but thanks to ABC, he can finally check that

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.440
one off his bucket list.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.280
Unfortunately, that’s not actually a good
thing.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.480
The Alec Baldwin Show is just not good.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:44.830
Talk show interviewing is a unique skill,
a host must ask simple but interesting questions

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:48.140
that set up their guests to speak at length
in an entertaining fashion.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:52.820
Unlike the established hosts who actually
excel at it, like Stephen Colbert and Jimmy

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.980
Kimmel, Baldwin frequently interrupts his
very patient guests so that he can tell a

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:59.760
story about his favorite subject: Alec Baldwin.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.240
It seems like audiences weren’t as into
Baldwin as he was himself, though.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:08.020
It did so badly that ABC moved it from its
prominent Sunday night time slot to bury it

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.630
in the hinterlands of Saturday.

